# Controllers design

Controllers holds the Fearless manager: a desktop program that keeps a set of project folders, compiles those projects, runs the mains of those projects, and connects those projects to the desktop and to Eclipse.
Next to the manager, and independent from the manager, Controllers also holds:
- `fearlessPluginProject`: the Eclipse plugin showing the projects of a manager inside Eclipse, with content assist (see Content assist).
- `agentTools`: a library driving the real pointer and keyboard, for GUI tests of any program.
- `benchmarks`: speed measures of Fearless programs against the runtime.

The manager is an island: the manager behaves the same on every system, and this is the objective of the manager.
When a part of the system (a tray, a dialog, a desktop shell) would make the manager behave differently than this design, the manager does not use that part that way.
When the system does not offer what the manager needs (a display, a file system feature the manager relies on, `.fearless`, or an extension that neither a compilation nor an `EditMetadata` brings (see Reconciling)), or the manager folder exists and does not hold what the manager wrote there, the manager stops with a 'fatal error' saying so.
This is the only concept of 'fatal error'.

A 'fatal error' is shown in a dialog listing the mains and the compilations that are currently running; with no display, the error goes to the standard error of the manager program instead, and no step below waits for a dialog.
A 'fatal error' ends the manager. This happens in order:
-The dialog shows. Nothing below happens until the dialog has been closed.
-All the running mains and compilations are terminated. The manager waits until every main and compilation has terminated.
-The manager deletes the manager folder.
-The manager removes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family (`.eradicateAll`, see ReconciliatorDesign).

Everything the manager is asked to do either happens or is refused with an error naming what was refused and the reason of the refusal; nothing is silently skipped.

## Program and manager folder

A manager is one program folder, `fearlessManaged<version>`, for example `fearlessManaged0_001`.
The 'launcher' of the manager is the executable file of the program folder that starts the manager program.
The manager folder of a manager is `fearless<version>`, next to the program folder, on every system. The manager folder holds everything the manager remembers; deleting the manager folder makes the manager start from nothing.
A manager folder that can not be created is a 'fatal error' naming the manager folder.

## Starting the 'single' manager
Starting the manager program leaves one message in the manager folder, or two for the path of a folder or of a `.fearless` file. Several OS processes running the manager can be alive at the same time, and one of them is the 'single' manager: the OS process running the manager that handles the messages.

The manager program takes:
- zero arguments: the message is `Show`;
- one argument starting with `FearlessCommand<Verb>`, a `call` (see Requests and messages): the message is the verb `<Verb>` with the arguments of the verb and the `port` that follow in the `call`;
- one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`: the path of a file or a folder, as the system passes the path when the file is opened (for example by a double click); the messages are `Register` with that path, then `Show`, for a folder or a `.fearless` file, and the message is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with that path otherwise.
For example:
```
"FearlessCommandQuit"
"FearlessCommandShow hello_world"
"FearlessCommandSelectMains hello_world hello.Hello1 hello.Hello3"
"FearlessCommandRun hello_world Port:51234"
"FearlessCommandRegister Base16:2F646174612F68656C6C6F"
```
(the last one registers `/data/hello` on Linux).
An OS process running the manager that is not the 'single' manager ends after writing its message files; an OS process running the manager that becomes the 'single' manager applies its own message files like every other message file.
An OS process running the manager started with two or more arguments, or with a bad argument, leaves the message `InvalidCall` with the details. When the 'single' manager handles `InvalidCall`, the 'single' manager shows the error in a dialog and continues running.
An OS process running the manager encountering a 'fatal error' while another process holds the lock of `instance.lock` (see The 'single' manager in Implementation notes) leaves the message `FatalError` with the details and ends normally.
The 'single' manager handles `FatalError` as a 'fatal error'.
An OS process running the manager encountering a 'fatal error' while no other process holds the lock of `instance.lock` handles that 'fatal error' as above.
An OS process running the manager encountering a 'fatal error' before that OS process running the manager knows the path of its manager folder (the program was not started by the launcher of a manager, or the system does not read file names as UTF-8) only shows the error, then ends: that OS process running the manager deletes no folder, leaves no message, and removes no setup.

The 'single' manager runs until Quit, or until a 'fatal error'.

Closing the manager window hides the manager window; an icon stays visible to show that the 'single' manager is still running.
On a system with a tray (a system tray or StatusNotifierItem), the icon is a tray icon.
Clicking the tray icon, with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon: Show manager and Quit manager.

On a system with no tray, the manager shows the icon itself.
The manager window is closed and replaced with the 'tray icon window': a window of the size of a tray icon, with no title bar and no border, holding only the Fearless icon, in the bottom right corner of the screen and always above the other windows.
Clicking the 'tray icon window', with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon.
For half a second after that menu shows, a press or a release on the entries of that menu does nothing.
An entry of that menu is chosen by a click: a press and a release of a button on that same entry.
Showing the manager removes the 'tray icon window' and shows the manager window.

## Requests and messages

Everything the manager is asked to do for a project is a request, and every request is one of the requests below, whether the request comes from a message or from a GUI operation.
The 'single' manager applies the requests one at a time, in the order they arrive, each one completely before the next.
Applying a `Run` or a `Compile` is starting the mains or the compilation; applying a `Terminate` ends once the JVM of the main has ended.

A message is the file `messages/<time>-<id>.msg` in the manager folder: `<time>` is the 20 digit count of milliseconds since 1970 when the message was written, and `<id>` is a random UUID. The 'single' manager applies the message files in the order of their names, and deletes each message file after reading it.
Only the manager program writes message files: the manager program writes `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp`, then renames `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp` to `messages/<time>-<id>.msg`, so the 'single' manager never reads a partial message. The 'single' manager deletes every `.tmp` file older than 60 seconds.
An OS process running the manager whose `.tmp` or `.msg` name already exists picks another `<id>`; the rename never replaces an existing file.
The 'single' manager relies on the system telling the 'single' manager when a file appears in `messages`. When an OS process running the manager becomes the 'single' manager, the 'single' manager writes a `.tmp` file in `messages` and waits for that report; no report within 5 seconds is a 'fatal error'. Some file systems, for example some network drives, do not report new files.
Every other program commanding the manager (the Eclipse plugin, a script, a test) runs the manager program with a `call` as its argument.
GUI operations that are requests command the manager the same way, through a `call`:
every such GUI operation has an equivalent message, and every message is either ill formed or has an equivalent GUI operation, except `Check`: the GUI offers `Check` only as the main button of a project that is not code or is 'invalid', and a `call` can send `Check` of any project.
A request changes files (those of the manager folder, those of a project folder, the associations of the system, or the plugin and `manager.info` of an Eclipse), starts or ends a main or a compilation, writes the Output of a project, shows a window of the manager (`Show`, and `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` when several mains claim the extension), or is `Quit`.
The GUI operations that do none of this are not requests and have no message: Docs, Base documentation, Browse and Logs, which open another program on a file, and the Clear button of an Output, which empties the Output. A GUI operation that finds nothing to open says so in a dialog.
The error of an ill formed message shows in a dialog of the manager window, and on the port when the `call` has a `port`. A hidden manager window is shown first.
Showing the manager window asks the desktop to bring the manager window to the front. A desktop that keeps windows from taking the focus, such as GNOME, may leave the manager window behind the window the user is working in; such a desktop tells the user in its own way, for example with a notification that Fearless Manager is ready.

A message is a verb, then the arguments of the verb and the `port`, one per line.
A verb is one of the following, written in upper camel case. Verbs about the whole manager:
- `Show`: show the manager window; with the name of a registered project as its argument, also select that project;
- `Register <path>`: register the folder, or the folder a file is in, then select that project; a folder already registered is only selected;
- `Quit`: end the manager and every main and compilation the manager runs;
- `Reset`: end the manager as a 'fatal error' ends the manager (the steps at the top of this document), with no dialog;
- `ConnectEclipse <path>`: connect the Eclipse found from the path, then say so in a dialog, asking to restart Eclipse if Eclipse is running and to open the Fearless perspective;
- `EditMetadata <path>`: replace `projects.info` with the content of the file at the path (see Registered projects for when the edit is refused);
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`: open the file with the main claiming the extension of the file; when several mains claim that extension, show a window listing those mains (see Opening a file). Double clicking the file on the desktop is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`.
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`: open the file with the main `<main>` of the project `<name>`, one of the mains claiming the extension of the file. Picking a main in the window that `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` shows is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`.
Verbs about one project, taking the name of a registered project:
- `Run <name>`: select the project and run the selected mains of the project, in the order of the selected mains;
- `Compile <name>`: select the project and compile the project if the project is 'out of date'; otherwise the Output says `The project "hello" was already up to date and compiled.`, and so does the connection of the `call`, if the `call` has a `port`;
- `Check <name>`: select the project and report in the Output of the project the 'problem' of the project, or that the project is 'out of date', or that the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date';
- `Terminate <name>`: select the project and end the main of the project currently running, if any;
- `DeleteCache <name>`: select the project and delete the compilation cache of the project;
- `Kind <name> <kind>`: set the kind of the project to `<kind>`;
- `Forget <name>`: forget the project;
- `Mount <name> <type> <data>`: set the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project to the data project `<data>` (see DataReadDataEditDesign);
- `Unmount <name> <type>`: remove the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project;
- `Attenuate <name> <main> <attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`: set the attenuators of the main `<main>` of the project to the attenuators `<attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`, applied in order; no `<attenuator>` removes all the attenuators of `<main>` (see the Manager section of AttenuationDesign);
- `SelectMains <name> <main1>..<mainn>`: set the selected mains of the project to the mains given; no `<main>` removes the selected mains;
`Forget`, `Kind`, `Mount`, `Unmount`, `Attenuate` and `SelectMains` are special cases of `EditMetadata`: each one is `EditMetadata` with the current `projects.info` changed in one place (the project removed, the `kind` of the project replaced, the mount of one DataRead type replaced or removed, the attenuators of one main replaced, the selected mains of the project replaced).
Such a verb is applied, or is ill formed with the same error, exactly as that `EditMetadata` is; `Mount` is also ill formed when `<data>` is not the name of a registered project.
Before applying the request of a call with a `port`, the manager connects to that port of this machine.
What the manager sends on the connection starts with a line saying what became of the message: `Applied` or `Refused`.
After `Refused`, the manager sends the error, as the dialog shows the error, and ends the connection.
After `Applied`, the manager ends the connection at once, except for `Run` and `Compile`.
For `Run` and `Compile`, the connection also receives the Output of the mains or of the compilation: after `Applied`, the manager sends on the connection what the running mains or the compilation print, from their start and then as they print, with the marks of the Output (see Running mains and compilations).
The connection ends when the last main or the compilation ends, at once when no main and no compilation starts.
`Quit` and `Reset` may end the manager before the manager sends `Applied`.
The connection is how another program (Eclipse, a running main) knows that the manager has applied the message of that program, and follows the mains or the compilation that program asked for.
Two more message files exist, `InvalidCall <details>` and `FatalError <details>`, written only by an OS process running the manager, about a failure of that OS process running the manager. `InvalidCall` and `FatalError` are not verbs: no `call` and no GUI operation names those messages.
The details of `InvalidCall` and `FatalError` are every line after the first line, any number of lines.

```
call ::= command port?
command ::= FearlessCommandShow projectName?
  | FearlessCommandQuit
  | FearlessCommandReset
  | FearlessCommandRegister base16
  | FearlessCommandConnectEclipse base16
  | FearlessCommandEditMetadata base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16 projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandRun projectName
  | FearlessCommandCompile projectName
  | FearlessCommandCheck projectName
  | FearlessCommandTerminate projectName
  | FearlessCommandDeleteCache projectName
  | FearlessCommandKind projectName kind
  | FearlessCommandForget projectName
  | FearlessCommandMount projectName typeName projectName
  | FearlessCommandUnmount projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandAttenuate projectName typeName typeName1..typeNamen
  | FearlessCommandSelectMains projectName typeName1..typeNamen
kind ::= idle | code | dataRead | dataEdit
port ::= Port:1 .. Port:65535
projectName ::= (?!(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])$)[a-z_][a-z0-9_]{0,190}
typeName ::= (?!(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])[.])[a-z][a-z0-9_]*[.]_*[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*'*
```
The `typeName1..typeNamen` of `FearlessCommandSelectMains` are all different.
The `base16` of a `call` names an absolute path that is not the root of a drive.
An argument starting with `FearlessCommand` is a bad argument when the argument is not a `call`.

Ill formed messages:
- a port where no program of this machine accepts the connection;
- a name that is not the name of a registered project; the error lists the registered projects, except for the `<name>` of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`, whose error lists the mains claiming the extension of the file (see the `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` entry below);
- a `Register` whose path names nothing, or whose folder (the path, or the folder holding the file of the path) is the manager folder, anything inside the manager folder or a folder holding the manager folder, or is inside the folder of a registered project or holds the folder of a registered project;
- a `ConnectEclipse` whose path leads to no Eclipse folder, and an `EditMetadata` whose path names no file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` whose path names no file, names an empty file, or names a file whose extension no main claims;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` where `<main>` of `<name>` is not one of the mains claiming the extension of the file, also when `<name>` is not a registered project (the error lists the mains claiming the extension);
- an `EditMetadata` that Registered projects refuses;
- `Run` and `Compile` of a project that is not code, naming the kind of the project;
- `Run` of a project whose `attenuators` name a main that is not a main of the project, or an attenuator that is not an attenuator of the project (the error lists the mains, or the attenuators);
- `Run` of a project with a broken mount, or with a DataRead type with no mount (see DataReadDataEditDesign; the error lists each broken mount, naming what makes that mount broken, and each DataRead type with no mount);
- `Run` and `Compile` of an 'invalid' project (the error is the 'problem' of the project);
- `Run` of a project that is not compiled, naming the state of the project; `Run` of a project with no selected main, or with a selected main that is not a main of the project (the error lists the mains of the project);
- `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` or `DeleteCache` while a main or a compilation of the project is currently running;
- `Terminate` while a compilation of the project is currently running;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` that runs a main of a project where a `Run` of only that main is ill formed (the error is the error of that `Run`).

Whether a message is ill formed is decided when the 'single' manager starts applying the request of the message, after the 'autoCheck' (see Project states) of the project the request runs on, from the message and the state that 'autoCheck' finds, before anything is compiled, run or written to the Output.
The error of an ill formed message is never in the Output: the Output of a project holds only the results of well formed requests. A failing compilation is the result of a well formed request: the request is right, the code is broken.

## Info files

The files the manager writes for the manager and for Eclipse (`projects.info`, `manager.info`) are Info files: text in the Fearless character set (the characters a Fearless `Str` allows).
An Info is a string `"..."`, a list `[a, b]`, or an object `{"key": value, ...}` whose keys are distinct strings.
Inside an Info string, `\"` is a quote, `\\` a backslash and `\n` a newline; there is no other escape: `"\t"` is an invalid Info.

A text that may hold characters outside the Fearless character set is a `taggedText`:
```
taggedText ::= Str:str | UStr:uStr | base16
base16 ::= Base16:([0-9A-F][0-9A-F])+
str ::= strAtom strMore1..strMoren [ ]*
strMore ::= [ ]*[+] strAtom | [ ]*[|^] strAtom?
strAtom ::= [ ]*"[^"\n]*" | [ ]*`[^`\n]*` | [ ]*[(] str [)]
uStr ::= uStrAtom uStrMore1..uStrMoren [ ]*
uStrMore ::= [ ]*[+] uStrAtom | [ ]*[|^] uStrAtom? | [ ]*[.]u codePoints
codePoints ::= [ ]*["]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*["] | [ ]*[`]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*[`]
codePoint ::= [0-9A-F]{1,6}
uStrAtom ::= strAtom [.]u codePoints? | [ ]*[(] uStr [)]
```
Every character of a `str` and of a `uStr` is in the Fearless character set; parentheses nest at most 100 deep.
Each `codePoint` is at most `10FFFF` and not from `D800` to `DFFF`.
The text of `Str:str` is the `Str` the Fearless expression `str` evaluates to, and the text of `UStr:uStr` is the `UStr` the Fearless expression `uStr` evaluates to (see `Str` and `UStr` in the standard library: a literal holds its characters as they are, so `\n` inside a literal is two characters).
The text of `base16` is the bytes the current file system interprets as a path; only a path is a `base16`.
For example:
- `"C:\data\hello"` is the path `C:\data\hello`, written in an Info as `"path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\hello\""`;
- `"Hello"|"World"` is `Hello\nWorld`, encoded as `"Str:\"Hello\"|\"World\""`;
- `"caf".u"00E9"` is `caf` followed by the character `00E9`, encoded as `"UStr:\"caf\".u\"00E9\""`.
The manager writes `Str:` then `.escape` of the text when the text is a `Str`; `UStr:` then `.escape` of the text when the text is a `UStr` and not a `Str`; the `base16` of the path only when the text is a path that is neither a `Str` nor a `UStr`.

## Registered projects

The manager folder holds `projects.info`, the record of every registered project (the path, the kind, the selected mains, the mounts and the attenuators) and the system extensions.
The manager is the only program writing `projects.info`: the manager writes the new content to another file in the manager folder, then renames that file to `projects.info`, so a reader such as Eclipse never reads a partial `projects.info`.

`projects.info` is an Info file: an object with `projects`, the list of the registered projects in the order they were registered, and `systemExtensions`:
```
{
  "projects": [
    {
      "path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\01_hello_world\"",
      "kind": "code",
      "mains": ["hello.Hello1", "hello.Hello3"],
      "mounts": {"hello.Diary": "diary"},
      "attenuators": {"hello.Hello1": ["base.NoManagerQuit", "hello.NoGui"]}
    },
    {
      "path": "UStr:\"C:\\Users\\Jos\".u\"00E9\"+(\"\\da\".u\"1F600\")+(\"ta\".u)",
      "kind": "dataEdit"
    }
  ],
  "systemExtensions": ["htm"]
}
```
- `systemExtensions`: the system extensions this manager takes, whether or not a main claims those extensions; each one a system extension (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign), listed once.
Each project has `path` and `kind`, and may have `mains`, `mounts` and `attenuators`; an absent field is the empty list or the empty object:
- `path`: the absolute path of the project folder, any path the system allows, exactly as the current system and file system name the folder, separators included, as a `taggedText`. Two projects never share a folder, and a project folder is never inside another project folder.
A project folder is never the root of a drive, the manager folder, a folder inside the manager folder or a folder holding the manager folder.
- `kind`: `idle`, `code`, `dataRead` or `dataEdit`.
- `mains`: the selected mains, set by `SelectMains`.
- `mounts`: for each DataRead type, the name of the data project mounted on that DataRead type (see DataReadDataEditDesign).
- `attenuators`: for each main the attenuators the manager applies to that main, in order; a main absent from `attenuators` gets no attenuator. Naming a main or an attenuator the project does not have is well formed.

`projects.info` holds no project name: the name of a project is the name of the marker in the project folder (see Registering a folder).
The 'single' manager keeps the names in memory.
A 'name lookup' gives a name to every project of `projects.info`, in the order of `projects.info`: the name of each project is the result of name inference (see Registering a folder), which renames or creates the marker when needed, so no project takes a name of a project before it in `projects.info`.
A 'name check' of a project compares the marker in the project folder with the name in memory; when they differ, the manager does a 'name lookup'.
The manager does a 'name lookup' at start; before applying a request, the manager does a 'name check' of every project the request names, and a 'name lookup' when the request names a name that is not the name of a project; an 'autoCheck' of a project starts with a 'name check' of the project.
A project folder the manager can not reach keeps the name the manager knew, and gets no name at start; the manager forgets that project (see Project states).

The `mains`, `mounts` and `attenuators` of a project stay when the kind of the project changes, and a mount stays when the data project of the mount is forgotten: a later `Kind` back to `code`, or a project registered under the name of that data project, makes them work again. For example, a code project swaps real data and test data by registering one folder or the other under the name of the data project.

A `projects.info` that is not a valid Info file, or does not hold what is above, is a 'fatal error' pointing at the offending text in the Frontend error message style.

The whole `projects.info` can be edited by hand from the GUI, or via an `EditMetadata` naming a file (outside of the manager folder) with the intended new content.
The editor of the GUI remembers the content the editor opened; when the user commits, and `projects.info` does not hold that content, the editor refuses the commit in a dialog, and the user edits the new content again; otherwise the commit is an `EditMetadata`.
A project of the edit is a project of `projects.info` when both have the same `path`.
An edit is refused, and nothing changes, when:
- the whole text is not valid: the error points at the offending text in the Frontend error message style;
- the edit removes a project, or changes the kind of a project, while a main or a compilation of that project is currently running;
- the edit changes the mounts of a project, or removes a data project mounted on a DataRead type of that project, or changes the kind or the folder of that data project, while a main of that project is currently running;
- the edit adds to `systemExtensions` an extension reconcile refuses (see Refusals in ReconciliatorDesign);
- the edit removes from `systemExtensions` an extension that a main of a project claims, while a main or a compilation of that project is currently running.
An accepted edit applies at once: projects the edit removes are forgotten, projects the edit adds are registered as they are, the compilation cache (`Coordinator.deleteCache`) of each added project, of each project whose kind changes and of each project whose mains claim an extension the edit removes from `systemExtensions` is deleted, and every project gets an 'autoCheck'.



## Registering a folder

The name of a project is the name of the marker of the project: the only file with the `.fearless` extension at the top of the project folder whose name does not start with `.`, `<name>.fearless`. A project name follows the rules of a single file name segment of a Fearless project (lowercase letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit, not a reserved name such as `con`).

Registering a folder that is not registered already adds the project at the end of `projects.info`, named by name inference.
Name inference: The name is the name of the first marker of the folder in file name order, when that name is valid and no project before this project in `projects.info` uses that name.
Otherwise the name is the folder name turned into a valid name: lowercased, every sequence of other characters turned into `_`, a leading `p` when the result starts with a digit, then the first of `name`, `name2`, `name3`... not used by a project before this project in `projects.info`. The first marker is renamed (or the marker is created) to match.
If present, the compilation cache (`.fearless_out`) of the newly registered folder is deleted (`Coordinator.deleteCache`).
`.fearless_out` belongs to the Coordinator in every registered folder, whatever the kind of the project.
The newly registered project is in the `idle` state.

Forgetting a project asks no confirmation.
Forgetting a project removes the project from `projects.info`; nothing in the project folder is changed.

## Project states

Each project is in one of these states, shown as a badge on the tile of the project:
- `code: invalid content`, `dataRead: invalid content`, `dataEdit: invalid content`: the file names of the project are not valid Fearless file names (for code, also the checks of `Coordinator.check`, see CoordinatorDesign), the project folder holds more than one top level `.fearless` file, or the folder `.config/icon` holds more than one PNG file or a PNG file that can not be read;
- `idle`;
- `dataRead`, `dataEdit`;
- `code: compilation cache out of date`: the project is 'out of date';
- `code: compiled`: the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date';
- `code: busy`: a main or a compilation of the project is currently running.

A project in a state `<kind>: invalid content` is 'invalid'. The 'problem' of an 'invalid' project is the error naming which of the causes above holds.
An 'autoCheck' of a project reads the project folder and finds the state of the project and the mains of the project.
An 'autoCheck' that finds the project 'invalid' while no compilation of the project runs writes the 'problem' into `.fearless_out/_error.txt` with `Coordinator.writeError`, for every kind of project.
An 'autoCheck' that can not reach the project folder terminates the main or the compilation of the project, if running, and every running main of a project with a mount of that project, and shows a dialog with the error
`The project "hello" connects to the folder "/home/user/hello".`
`This folder is now unreachable.`
`The project "hello" has now been removed.`
then the 'autoCheck' is a `Forget` of the project.

An 'autoCheck' of a code project is a call of `Coordinator.check` (see CoordinatorDesign), which gives the state of the project: 'invalid' with the 'problem', 'out of date' (`State.outOfDate`), or `State.compiled`. The manager checks the marker and the icon of the project before calling `Coordinator.check`.

The mains of a code project are known when `Coordinator.check` gives `State.compiled`: the `.mains` of the `.manifest` of `State.compiled`.

The manager is 'active' while the manager window is shown, and 'passive' while the manager window is hidden (the tray icon or the 'tray icon window' is shown) or minimized.
A 'passive' manager becomes 'active' when the manager shows an error, or when the user shows the manager window.
An 'active' manager notices changes made on disk by other programs: every three seconds the 'active' manager runs an 'autoCheck' of the selected project, and in turn of one other project, and updates states, badges and buttons, including moving a project in and out of 'invalid'. A project gets an 'autoCheck' when a main or a compilation of the project ends.
A 'passive' manager does not run an 'autoCheck' of the projects every three seconds.

The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no PNG file in `.config/icon`, an icon generated from the project name.

## General GUI shape
Dropping files or folders on the manager window is a `Register` of each file or folder dropped.
Every button is disabled for half a second after it is pressed.

A menu bar at the top, with the Manager menu: Add folder (`Register`, through a file picker), Edit project metadata (`EditMetadata`), Connect Eclipse (`ConnectEclipse`), Base documentation (`Coordinator.docs` of the package `base`, with any folder), Reset (`Reset`, after a confirmation dialog saying what Reset does, with the buttons Reset and Cancel, Cancel preselected), Quit (`Quit`).

A simplified 'desktop with icons' fills the manager window under the menu bar while no project is selected; while a project is selected, the simplified desktop takes the left side of a vertical divider, and the project area takes the right side.
Tiles represent the registered projects, sorted by name: each tile shows the project icon with the state badge of the project, and the project name under the project icon.
A busy project (compiling or running) shows an animated badge.
Clicking a tile is `Show` with the name of the project of the tile.
Double clicking the tile of a project in the state `code: compiled` is `Run` of that project; for a project with one main, the `Run` comes after a `SelectMains` naming that main. For any other project, and for a project with no main, or with several mains and no selected main, a double click is a click.

