- The Docs V button (enabled once the mains are known), opening a menu of the packages of the project, the `packages` of `State.compiled` (see CoordinatorDesign); choosing a package opens the documentation of that package (`Coordinator.docs`) with the program that opens `.html` files, which is the manager when `html` is a system extension.
- The Kind V button, showing the kind of the project, like `Kind: code`, and opening a menu with one entry for each kind the project is not: "Back to idle", "Become dataRead", "Become dataEdit" and "Become code".
- The Project V button, opening a menu with Browse, opening the project folder in the file explorer, Forget (`Forget`), and Delete cache (`DeleteCache`).
Kind V, Forget and Delete cache are greyed out while a main or a compilation of the project is currently running.
Under the header, a status line: the project icon, then the state of the project as Project states names it, followed, for an 'invalid' project, by the first line of the 'problem', for a busy project by what is running, and by a note for each broken mount of the project.

Pressing Run V opens a drop-down menu showing the mains of the project and the selected mains that are not mains of the project, each with a checkbox, the selected mains checked, and a Run button at the end of the list, disabled while no entry is checked or a checked entry is not a main of the project. Checking or unchecking an entry is a `SelectMains` naming the checked entries. That Run button is `Run` of the project; the Run button of a project with one main is a `SelectMains` naming that main, then `Run` of the project.
A project with selected mains that shows no Run V shows the selected mains in the project area, with a Remove button: a `SelectMains` naming no main.
  
For a code project whose mains have a `Shortcut`, after the header we see each shortcut file present in the project folder under the name the manager gives that shortcut file (see Shortcut files), and only those, as the simplified desktop shows a project: the icon of the `Shortcut`, with the name of the shortcut file under the icon.
Double clicking a shortcut file there is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with the path of that shortcut file: the very message a double click on that file on the desktop of the system sends.


For every project, we have the Output, a 'collapsible area': an area with vertical scroll bars and a max size of 1/2 of the height of the manager window; the Output has a Clear button on the title of the Output. What the main button produces always goes to the Output, and the Output keeps everything until the Output is cleared or the next `Run`, `Compile` or `Check` of the project starts.

For a code project, after the Output we see the Tests 'collapsible area', a tree like the JUnit view of Eclipse, as the last 'autoCheck' of the project found the unit test logs (`.out/logs/_base/unit_test_log$<time>.log`):
- one top level node for each unit test log written since the last `Run` of the project started, in the order the logs were written (none when that `Run` wrote no unit test log), or, before any `Run` of the project since the manager started, for the newest unit test log; the node names the main of that `Run` that was running when the log was written (or, with no such main, the time of the log), with the counts, like `hello.MyTests: 3 passed, 1 failed, 0 disabled`, and a green mark, or a red mark when a test case failed;
- under each top level node, one node per test case of the log, with the name of the test case and a green, red or grey mark for passed, failed or disabled;
- under a failed test case, the lines of the text of the failure.
A top level node with a failed test case, and a failed test case, start expanded; the other nodes start collapsed.

Every entry of `projects.info` about a project except `path` shows in the project area of that project, and every entry has a GUI operation that removes that entry.

For a code project, and for any project with `attenuators`, after the Output and the Tests we see the Attenuators 'collapsible area', one block per main: the attenuators applied to that main in order, each with Remove, Up and Down buttons, then a drop down of the attenuators of the project, with an Add button. Every change is an `Attenuate` of that main. An attenuator type or a main type that is not in the `.manifest` of the `State.compiled` of this project is displayed with a graphical mark, the same mark that flags projects whose file names are not valid Fearless file names.

For a code project whose mains claim extensions (`OpenWith`), after the Attenuators we see a 'collapsible area', one block per main claiming extensions:
  "Extensions this main opens:" (each extension with its icon),
  "Conflicting extensions:" (each extension another main also claims, naming that main).

For a code project, for a data project, and for any project with `mounts`, we see the Data 'collapsible area' (see DataReadDataEditDesign).

For a code project we have the Logs 'collapsible area', showing the log files the mains wrote in `.out/logs`, with a Logs button opening that folder in the file explorer.


## Running mains and compilations

A project runs at most one thing at a time: a compilation, or the mains of one `Run`, one after the other.
A compilation of a project runs from the start of `Coordinator.compile` to the end of the last step of What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`.
Everything a running main or a compilation prints goes to the Output, and the Output marks where each main and the compilation start and end, and how each of them ended, so that Eclipse and the user know when the mains of a `Run` or the compilation are over.

- Compile (code projects): the compiler runs in its own JVM.
- Run (code projects): each main runs in its own JVM (`Coordinator.run`, see CoordinatorDesign).
- Terminate ends the running main: the JVM of that main ends, and no further main of that `Run` starts.
- A main or a compilation that crashes, or that takes all the memory, ends alone: the manager and the mains and compilations of the other projects keep running.


### Files changing while a main runs
An 'autoCheck' of a project with a running main that finds the project 'invalid' or 'out of date' terminates that main with the error `Some files inside the project "hello" were updated while main "hello.Hello" was running.`
That error is shown in the Output of the project.


## File associations

On Windows and Linux the 'single' manager makes the launcher of the manager the program opening every 'captured extension', with `.reconcile` of the Reconciliator (see ReconciliatorDesign). The 'captured extensions' are the extensions the manager takes: `.fearless`, every extension in `systemExtensions`, and the Fearless extensions the mains of the projects claim.
- An OS process running the manager that becomes the 'single' manager, of any version, or a starting portable Fearless, takes `.fearless` from another program of the Fearless family holding `.fearless` (see ReconciliatorDesign); the `replaced` of that `.reconcile` removes the manager folder of that other program at once, when that other program is a manager: that other program never sees a manager folder deleted in part. A 'single' manager whose manager folder is deleted quits, ending the mains and the compilations of that manager, and changes nothing else on the system: the `.reconcile` of the program that took `.fearless` deletes the setup of this manager (see ReconciliatorDesign), and the next start of this manager starts from nothing.

## OpenWith and Shortcut

A main can ask the manager to make the desktop of the system start the main: on a double click on any file with a given extension (`OpenWith`), or on a small file the manager creates for the main (`Shortcut`).

```
Foo: Main, OpenWith[IconsFoo, "foo"]{ s -> ... }
Bar: Main, Shortcut[IconsBar, "fapp123"]{ s -> ... }
Baz: Main, OpenWith[IconsBaz]{ s -> ... }
Qux: Main, Shortcut[IconsQux]{ s -> ... }
```

`IconsFoo`, `IconsBar`, `IconsBaz` and `IconsQux` are types the compiler generates for image files recognised as autoloaded assets (here `_pkg/icons/foo.png` becomes `IconsFoo`). Those types can come from any package the main can see, including `base`.

Informally: `Foo` wants every `.foo` file to show the `IconsFoo` image and to open `Foo` on a double click. `Bar` wants a file `bar.fapp123` in the top level of the project of `Bar`, showing the `IconsBar` image, that starts `Bar` on a double click; that file carries no information, the file is only a handle to start the main, and the file keeps working when moved anywhere else.

Mechanically `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` are the same: both claim an extension for a main. `Shortcut` additionally creates the shortcut file.

### Base

```
OpenWith[I,E]: {}
OpenWith[I]: {}
Shortcut[I,E]: {}
Shortcut[I]: {}
```

`Shortcut` does not extend `OpenWith`.

A main can implement several of those four types at once, for example `OpenWith[Icon1,"a"]` and `OpenWith[Icon2,"b"]` to claim two extensions; the icons can differ or be the same. Several `Shortcut` produce several shortcut files for the same main. Implementing the same type twice (`Shortcut[I]` inherited along two paths) counts once.

### Well formedness

The Frontend checks the claims of every declaration as well formedness errors, before generating code: a declaration with `OpenWith`, `Shortcut` or `PreLoadIcon` among its supertypes also has `Main` among its supertypes; the icon is a type name with no generic arguments; the extension is a string literal type (`"txt"` and `` `txt` `` are the same extension) holding 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`, not `fearless`; the extension of a `Shortcut` is `fapp` and three digits, and the extension of an `OpenWith` is not; one main claims each extension at most once, across all the `OpenWith` and `Shortcut` of the main. The Coordinator checks that the icon is the type generated for an image file (see CoordinatorDesign). The exact rules and errors are those of `inject.Methods.checkClaims` of Frontend and of the doc comments of `OpenWith`, `Shortcut` and `PreLoadIcon` in `base`.

The two kinds of extension, Fearless extension and system extension, are in ReconciliatorDesign.

### What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`

Everything below runs in the manager after `Coordinator.compile` ends. When `Coordinator.compile` failed, only step 2 runs. A step that fails makes the compilation fail: the manager calls `Coordinator.writeError` with the error of that step (for a failing reconcile, see Reconciling), and of the steps after that step only step 2 runs.

1. Check that every `Shortcut` claim of `Coordinator.cachedMains` of the project gives a valid shortcut file name (see Shortcut files), and check that every system extension an `OpenWith` claims is in `systemExtensions` (see System extensions).
2. Rebuild the in memory map (see The in memory map).
3. Create the missing shortcut files.
4. Call `Coordinator.finish`.

### The in memory map

The manager keeps `extension -> list of (project, main, icon)` in memory only. The manager builds the map at start up, in step 2 of every compilation (see What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`), after every deletion of the `.fearless_out` of a project (the project then claims nothing), and after every `EditMetadata` that removes a `code` project.
A build reads `Coordinator.cachedMains` of every registered `code` project whose `Coordinator.compile` is not running, and keeps the entries of each project whose `Coordinator.compile` is running.
At start up, before building the map, the manager calls `Coordinator.dropUnfinished` of every registered `code` project.
When a method of `Coordinator` raises or gives `Error.cacheCorrupted` for a project, the manager ends the running main or compilation of that project, if any, calls `Coordinator.deleteCache` of that project, and writes the error with `Coordinator.writeError`.
An `Error.cacheCorrupted` of another project during step 2 of a compilation does not make that compilation fail.
Each operation after which the manager rebuilds the map ends only after the rebuild; the same holds for everything else the manager keeps in memory.

### Autoselected extensions

The Coordinator chooses the extension of a `Shortcut` or an `OpenWith` without extension (see `._autoselect` in CoordinatorDesign), asking the manager through the `takeFreeExtension` of `Coordinator.compile`.
The manager answers `takeFreeExtension` one call at a time.
The answer is the first extension of the list that no claim in the in memory map uses and that no call answered for a compilation that has not ended yet.

### Shortcut files

For each `Shortcut` claim, the manager creates a file `<name>.<extension>` in the top level of the project, next to the marker, holding a single new line, like the marker. `<name>` is the simple name of the main turned into a file name, the inverse of the rule autoloading uses to name the type of an image: each uppercase letter becomes `_` and the lowercase of that letter, except the first letter of the name, which only becomes lowercase. `FooBar` becomes `foo_bar`, `_Foo` becomes `_foo`, `Foo_1` becomes `foo_1`, `FooBAR` becomes `foo_b_a_r`.

The inverse does not exist for every type name: type names can end in primes (`Foo'`), and can contain `_` followed by a lowercase letter (`Foo_bar`), which autoloading never produces. Such a main, a main whose name turns into a Windows reserved name (`Con`), two mains of the project with a `Shortcut` giving the same file name and extension (`a.Bar` and `b.Bar`), or a main whose shortcut file name is the name of a top level file without extension (`readme` for `hello.Readme`), fail the compilation:
`Main "hello.Foo'" can not have a Shortcut: its name has no matching file name. A main with a Shortcut has a name that can be mapped to a file name like "FooBar" can be mapped to "foo_bar".`

The shortcut file is one of the `.files` of the project (see The data in CoordinatorDesign). The manager never deletes a shortcut file: the user may have copied or moved shortcut files anywhere. A shortcut file whose extension no main claims any more stays, and opening that shortcut file is an `OpenWithOrShortcut` of an extension no main claims (see Opening a file).

### Reconciling

After each rebuild of the in memory map, and in each `EditMetadata` that changes `systemExtensions`, with the `systemExtensions` of the edit, once every other check of the edit (see Registered projects) passes, before the edit applies, the manager calls reconcile with all the 'captured extensions', each with an icon, and with the window classes (see Desk ids and icons). An extension claimed by exactly one main uses the icon of that claim; `.fearless` uses the Fearless icon, `base.IconsFearless`; every other 'captured extension' uses the conflict icon: an extension claimed by several mains, and an extension of `systemExtensions` that no main claims.

A `reconcile` that refuses `.fearless`, or an extension that neither a compilation nor the `EditMetadata` of that `reconcile` brings, is a 'fatal error', and so is a `reconcile` that refuses because more than one identity of the Fearless family is on the system. A `reconcile` that refuses a new extension a compilation brings ends the running main or compilation of every project with a claim of that extension, if any, deletes the compilation cache (`Coordinator.deleteCache`) of each of those projects, and writes the error into each of those projects with `Coordinator.writeError`; such a `reconcile` in step 2 of the compilation of one of those projects also makes that compilation fail. The error names the failing extension, the main claiming the extension and the program holding the extension.

The manager settles conflicts among the mains of the projects of the manager (the conflict icon); `.reconcile` settles the extensions with the system and every other program (see Refusals in ReconciliatorDesign).

#### Desk ids and icons

The icon of an extension is `Coordinator.iconFiles` of the icon of the claim (see CoordinatorDesign), or the files of the `icons` folder of the manager folder for the Fearless icon and the conflict icon (see Conflict icon).
The desk id of a main (the `.deskId` of `Launch`, see CoordinatorDesign) is `<identity>-<hash>`: the identity of the manager (see ReconciliatorDesign), then the hash of the project folder and the name of the main.
Reconcile also takes the `.preloaded` icons of every main of `Coordinator.cachedMains` of every registered `code` project whose `Coordinator.compile` is not running, each as the window class `<desk id>-<icon name>`, named by the simple name of the main, with the project folder as `.target`.
The `programIcon` of the manager is the icon of the launcher of the manager.
On Windows a running main gives its windows the `.ico` of `Coordinator.iconFiles` directly.

### Opening a file

On `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` the 'single' manager reacts:

1. One main claims the extension: run that main as `Run` runs a selected main, leaving the selected mains as they are. The running main receives the file as its first input file (see Input files).
2. Several mains claim the extension: a small window lists those mains with their icons as `project::pkg.Main`, sorted. Picking an entry sends `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`, with the project and the main of that entry; that message runs the main as in 1. Closing the window runs nothing.
3. No main claims the extension: the `OpenWithOrShortcut` is ill formed (see Ill formed messages).

GNOME types every empty file as `application/x-zerosize` whatever the name of the file and never hands an empty file to the manager; refusing empty files everywhere (see Ill formed messages) makes Windows and the other desktops behave the same.

### Input files

A running main sees its input files through `System.inputCursor` of the standard library: the list of the files given to the program, in the order they arrived.
The list starts with the file opened by `OpenWithOrShortcut`, a document or a shortcut file alike, and starts empty for a main started by `Run`.
Dropping files on a window of a running Fearless program adds those files at the end of the list, in the order of the drop. A drop holding anything that is not a file, a folder for example, adds nothing and is refused.
`System.inputCursor` is a cursor on the first position of the list; `#` gives the file at that position, empty until a file arrives there, and `.next` the cursor on the next position. So a program sees a new file by asking again at the first position that was empty.
An input file offers the reading methods of the autoloaded `TxtFile` and `ImageFile` assets, without their `AssetReadCapability` argument: the input file is itself the capability. Every read reads the file again, and a file that can not be read any more is a non deterministic error.

### Messages from a running main

A running main sends messages to the manager through `System.manager` of the standard library, the capability `CapManager`.
Each method of `CapManager` sends one message, as Eclipse does: the method listens on a port, runs the manager program with a `call` ending with that port, and returns when the manager ends the connection.
So a method of `CapManager` returns once the manager has applied the message of the method: `.run` and `.compile` return once the mains or the compilation end, and return the Output of the mains or of the compilation, the text the manager sent on the port after the line `Applied`; the other methods return `Void`.
A kind is a method of its own (`.kindIdle`, `.kindCode`, `.kindDataRead`, `.kindDataEdit`): a message of a running main can not hold a kind that is not one of the four.
`Mount` is `.mount(name, type, data)`, and `Unmount` is `.unmount(name, type)`.
A path is the list of the bytes naming the path, the bytes of the `Base16:` form of the path. A method whose arguments do not make a `call` (a path that is not absolute or is the root of a drive, a name that is not a `projectName`, a main, a type or an attenuator that is not a `typeName`, `.selectMains` with two equal mains) is a deterministic error of the running main: no message is sent.
A refused message makes the method a deterministic error holding the text after the line `Refused`.
`.quit`, `.reset`, and `.terminate` naming the project of the running main never return: the running main ends.

### System extensions

The error of step 1 for a system extension not in `systemExtensions` names the main, the claim and the extension. The manager window then asks in a dialog whether to add the extension to `systemExtensions`. Confirming the dialog is an `EditMetadata` adding the extension.

### Conflict icon

The conflict icon is the Fearless icon with a purple `?` on top of the Fearless icon, stored as a PNG in the standard library at `base/icons/conflict.png`. The manager reads the conflict icon and the Fearless icon, `base.IconsFearless`, from the folder of `base` (see The data in CoordinatorDesign) and materialises both icons like any other icon, the PNG and, on Windows, the `.ico`, in the `icons` folder of the manager folder.

The conflict icon is the autoloaded type `base.IconsConflict`. `base` autoloads the assets of `base` as if the files of `base` were under a `_base` folder: `.path` is `fear:/_base/icons/conflict.png` and `.diskPath` is `icons/conflict.png`, relative to the folder of `base`, which a running main receives as `fearlessBase.dir`.

## Eclipse

Connect Eclipse asks for a file or a folder through a file picker. The Eclipse folder is the one folder holding `.eclipseproduct`; the chosen file or folder is the Eclipse folder, is directly inside the Eclipse folder, is directly inside a folder directly inside the Eclipse folder, or is a folder holding only the Eclipse folder.
Connect Eclipse puts the plugin in the Eclipse folder, with `manager.info` next to the plugin: `manager.info` holds the path of the manager program folder, as a `taggedText`. From that path the plugin finds the manager program, the manager folder and the base cache (see CoordinatorDesign).

The Output of a running program has no bound on its size, so the manager does not pass it through files: Eclipse listens on a port, and the `Run` and `Compile` of Eclipse carry that port.
Eclipse shows a refused `Run` or `Compile` only through the dialog of the manager: the Output view stays empty.
Eclipse follows the mains or the compilation of the last `Run` or `Compile` of Eclipse; Eclipse sends no other `Run` or `Compile` until those mains or that compilation end, except for a save. Everything else Eclipse shows (the error of the last compilation, the unit test log) is read from files.

Saving a file of a code project in Eclipse compiles the project:
- while Eclipse follows a compilation of that project, the save is ignored: the files stay unsaved in the editors;
- otherwise Eclipse sends `Terminate` of the project (a running main is ended), saves every open file of the project together, and sends `Compile` of the project, or `Check` for a code project Eclipse sees as 'invalid', as Build does. During a compilation of that project that Eclipse does not follow, the manager refuses that `Terminate` and that `Compile` or `Check`, Eclipse saves the files anyway, and the project is 'out of date' once the running compilation ends (see `.compile` in CoordinatorDesign).

The error file `_error.txt` is a 'stale error file' once the project folder, a file or folder of the project whose path holds no file name or folder name starting with `.`, the folder `.config`, the folder `.config/icon` or a file of `.config/icon` has a time after the time of the error file and not after the current time. A 'stale error file' is shown nowhere.
The plugin names each registered project by the first top level `.fearless` file of its folder in file name order, and mirrors no folder without one.
The plugin mirrors every registered project into the Eclipse workspace, in the Fearless perspective only, and writes into a project folder only the files and folders the user saves or creates; a project the manager forgets is removed from the workspace. A workspace project named like a registered project that is not the mirror of that project is an error. Every two seconds the plugin reflects `projects.info` and the `.fearless_out` folders: the error of `_error.txt` (see CoordinatorDesign) in the Problems view, on the file and at the line the error names, or on the project while that file does not exist or the error names no file, while `_error.txt` is not a 'stale error file', and in the JUnit view the unit test log the running main writes in `.out/logs`: the complete records of the log, the lines of the log up to the last newline, without a test case whose closing line is not written yet. The plugin shows the followed mains or compilation in the Output view, emptied when the mains of a `Run` or a compilation start, like the Output area of the manager: a view with no buttons, where a stack frame and the header of a compile error are links opening that file at that line. Fearless files show with syntax highlighting. Build (`Compile` for a code project that is not 'invalid', `Check` for any other), Clean (`DeleteCache`), the main button below, the kind switches of the Fearless menu of a project, New Fearless Project (`Register`), Show in manager (`Show` with the name of the project) and Forget project are messages to the manager.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds the main button of the manager for the project of the active editor or of the selection. The main button shows what pressing the main button does, each with an image of its own: Check, Compile, Run, Run V, or Terminate while Eclipse follows the mains of a `Run` of that project, and is greyed out while Eclipse follows a compilation of that project. Run V asks which mains to run, with the selected mains checked, then sends `SelectMains` naming the mains chosen and `Run`. Besides the main button, the toolbar of the Fearless perspective holds only the buttons of text editing (Save, Undo, Redo, Search, the toggles of the editor and the navigation in the text); a button that does nothing for Fearless or repeats another is hidden, and so is the Run menu, in the Fearless perspective only. Eclipse finds the state from the files, with the rules of an 'autoCheck': the kind in `projects.info`, the marker, and the rules of `Coordinator.check` (see CoordinatorDesign). A code project 'invalid' for a cause Eclipse does not read shows Compile, and the manager refuses that `Compile` with the 'problem' of the project.
The toolbar of the Fearless perspective also holds New Package and New Type, for the project of the active editor or of the selection; each asks for a path inside the project folder and refuses a path that is not valid, naming the rule of the Fearless file names the path breaks:
- New Package takes a path like `lib/_beer`, and makes the package folder `_beer` with the folders holding the package folder;
- New Type takes a path like `lib/_beer/point`, and makes the file `point.fear` holding the type `Point`. The option Rank file makes the rank file of that package instead, `_rank_app.fear` or the rank chosen, holding a `use` line for each of `base.Main`, `base.UnitTests`, `base.Test`, `base.Void`, `base.Bool`, `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float`, `base.Str`, `base.List`, `base.Lists`, `base.Opt`, `base.Opts`, `base.Block`, `base.Error` and `base.Debug`. The option Example tests adds a test suite checking some arithmetic.
New Folder and New File of the Fearless perspective warn when the name is not a valid file name of a Fearless project, naming the rule the name breaks, and create the folder or the file anyway.
New Fearless Project registers a folder, made if missing (`Register`). New Fearless Project can first write a demo into a new or empty folder, then make the project code (`Kind`) once the manager lists the project: Hello world; Simple GUI, a window with a button; Shortcut and OpenWith, a main started by the shortcut file of the main and a main claiming an extension, each with an icon of its own. The OpenWith main of this demo is a window showing the text of the file the main is opened with; every file dropped on that window adds the text of that file at the end.
A missing or malformed file of the manager is an error shown in Eclipse, and the plugin stops following the manager until Eclipse starts again.

## Content assist

Content assist works in a Fearless file of a package folder of a mirrored project, after a dot and on request. Content assist uses the compiled information of the last compilation of the project and of the base cache: the `.types` of the `<pkg>.json` of every package, the `<pkg>.txt` of the package of each proposal (see CoordinatorDesign), and the map directives of the rank files of the project. A malformed `<pkg>.json`, a missing `<pkg>.txt`, and a public type or a method of a type missing from the documentation are errors: the content assist popup shows the error, with one proposal, Clean, which sends `DeleteCache` of the project. When `.fearless_out` holds `_unfinished.txt` before content assist reads the files, or, after content assist reads the files, holds `_unfinished.txt` or no longer holds a `<pkg>.json` that content assist read, a missing `<pkg>.txt` and a type or a method missing from the documentation are not errors: content assist shows its proposals without documentation. In a project that is 'invalid', content assist reads no rank file: no map directive and no `use` directive.

Content assist proposes:
- nothing while the cursor is inside a comment or a string, or touches a name that follows no dot (`ps|`, `Perso|`), except a type name being typed after `Name:` in a declaration;
- after a dot, or after a space following an expression, the methods of the type of the expression before the cursor, dot methods and operators alike: dot methods first, then by name and number of parameters; every method is proposed, also several with the same name and number of parameters (methods differing in the reference capability of the receiver). A method name being typed (`.fl|`, `+|`) keeps the methods starting with that name and is replaced by the chosen method; otherwise the chosen method replaces the dot, or is inserted at the cursor. An operator is inserted with a space on each side;
- after a lowercase name and a dot (`base.|`), and inside a qualified type name being typed (`base.Li|`, in code or in a `use` directive), the public types of the compiled package that name stands for whose simple name starts with what follows the dot.
- after `Name:` in a declaration, and while a simple type name is being typed there, the type expected at the cursor and the types without generics having that type as a direct supertype (the public types of the compiled packages, and the declarations of the edited file), whose simple name starts with what is typed.

When content assist has nothing to propose, content assist shows no popup.

After `(` and `,`, and on request, content assist also shows the type expected at the cursor as a hint, like `expected: Nat`. The type expected at the cursor is the type of the parameter of the argument of the call holding the cursor, or the result type of the method whose body holds the cursor.

Each proposal shows the signature of the method or type, with simple type names and with reference capabilities, and the documentation of the method or type.

The types of content assist are a best effort, not the typing of the compiler: the rules below say what content assist infers at least.
The text is read with the tokens of the compiler. The expression before the cursor is what follows the last `;`, `,`, `->`, `:` or the `'` starting a self name in the innermost bracket around the cursor, read with the grammar of the compiler: an atom, then posts left to right, where a post without parenthesis takes one atom, and `.m x = e rest` binds `x`. A bracket not closed yet runs to the end of the text. What the compiler would reject is unknown, and unknown has no methods. The type of:
- a type name is that type: a simple name is the alias of a `use` directive of the rank file of the package, else a type of the package; a name without `[..]` has no generics. A package name written in the package, in a qualified name or a `use` directive, stands for the package the `map` directives of the project give that name there. A number or a string is a `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float` or `base.Str`;
- a name is the type of what binds the name. The literals around the cursor bind the self name of the literal, or `this` for a top level declaration, the generics of a declaration and of the method holding the cursor, and the parameters of that method: to the written type, else to the type of the parameter of the method the literal implements (the method of that name, or, for a method without name, the only abstract method with that number of parameters not implemented by name in the literal). An earlier `x = e` of the same expression binds `x` to the first parameter of the continuation of the method of that `x = e`; inside that `e`, `x` is unknown;
- a literal is the type the literal declares, or the type written before the literal, or else the type expected by the call the literal is an argument of, or the result type of the method the literal is the whole body of; the generics of that type still open are bound by the types of the bodies of the methods of the literal;
- a call is the result types of every method of that name and number of arguments of the type of the receiver of the call (several when those methods differ in the reference capability of the receiver, or when the supertypes of a type the last compilation does not know disagree), each result type with the unknown generics, of the method and of the type of the receiver, bound by what the call shows: the explicit type arguments, the arguments meeting the types of the parameters, and the type expected of the call. An expression of several types has the methods of all those types.

A type declared in the edited file that the last compilation does not know has every method of the supertypes of that type: when supertypes have methods with the same name and number of parameters, all those methods are proposed.

## Error management
An error of the Coordinator is in Coordinator (`Report`, `Violation`); an error of the manager is in Controllers, in the same (`Report`, `Violation`) classification.

## Future design

What the design will cover later; nothing in this section is current behaviour.
- The message files are replaced by a channel that does not depend on the file system reporting new files, for example a local socket of the 'single' manager.
- The Output keeps only the last part of what a running main or a compilation prints, so a program printing without end does not fill the memory.
- The GUI becomes a separate program, as for many Linux daemons: the manager has no window, and the GUI commands the 'single' manager through the manager program with a `call`, as every other program does. The GUI could be a Fearless program with some extra permissions.
- A later double click on a file for a main already running adds that file to the input files of the running main.
- A double click for a main already running signals the running main to wake up, for example bringing the window of the running main to the front.
- A 'captured extension' that no main claims gets an icon of its own, showing that the manager holds the extension, instead of the conflict icon.
- Removing or changing a data project mounted on a DataRead type of a code project shows a confirmation dialog in the GUI, naming the code projects that get a broken mount.
- A data project folder can be deleted when the folder is left empty, and so can a zip, since a zip is a folder for a main. Deleting something and writing it again "the same" can then give another structure on disk: what was a zip can come back as a plain folder. How to handle this is to be discussed.
- A main learns the extensions the main claims, for example through a `System` method. A main with `OpenWith[I]` gets an extension such as `ffile042`, chosen by the manager and varying between machines and compile orders; to save a document the user can later double click, the program must name the document `<something>.ffile042`.

## Implementation notes

What a rewrite needs that neither this design nor the other repositories say.

An implementation decides alone every detail the design leaves open below the concepts of the design: wire formats, encodings, intervals, the library calls, the content of the files the design names (information for Eclipse can be added to `manager.info`). An implementation never adds what the user or another program can observe and the design does not describe: a file in the manager folder or in a project, a verb, a GUI element, a message, a fallback. Those go into the design first.

- The stand-in for the tray: under Wayland a program can not place the windows of the program, but AWT on Linux is an X11 client (through XWayland under Wayland), so the manager places the window itself.
  The stand-in is an AWT window of type popup. On X11 the window manager does not manage a popup window: no taskbar lists a popup window, and a popup window stays where the manager places the popup window.
- A double click on a button presses the button twice, and the second press comes before the request of the first press changes the window, so the second press sends the same request again.
- A main or a compilation ending alone: the JVM of each main and of each compilation is a process of its own. A process that crashes ends alone. The out of memory killer of the Linux kernel ends one process at a time, chosen by memory use, and Windows ends no group of processes either. systemd-oomd ends a whole cgroup when the memory pressure stays high; a cgroup is a group of processes the Linux kernel accounts together, and a child process starts in the cgroup of the parent process. So on Linux with systemd as init, `Fs.processBuilder` starts each child with `systemd-run --user --scope`, in a transient scope: a cgroup of its own.
  systemd-oomd is on by default on Ubuntu with GNOME, Fedora (GNOME and COSMIC) and Omarchy. systemd-oomd is off on Arch, and missing on Debian, Kubuntu, Lubuntu, Xubuntu and openSUSE Tumbleweed. Void has no systemd.
- A main or a compilation ending with the manager: the JVM of each main and of each compilation reads its standard input, a pipe the manager holds open, and halts when that standard input ends. So a manager that ends, however the manager ends, ends every main and every compilation the manager started.
- Removing a manager folder at once: rename the manager folder to a new name in the same folder, then delete the renamed folder. On Windows a folder holding a file that a process has open can not be renamed, and the 'single' manager holds `instance.lock` open: how to remove the folder of a running manager at once on Windows is open.
- The 'single' manager: the process holding the lock (`FileChannel.tryLock`) of the file `instance.lock` in the manager folder. The operating system releases the lock when the process ends, however the process ends, so a manager that was killed leaves no stale lock.

- Tray on Linux: `java.awt.SystemTray` needs an XEmbed tray, which GNOME and most Wayland desktops do not have; what those desktops show is a StatusNotifierItem on the session bus, registered with `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher`, the menu of the item served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu`. A desktop with no owner of the watcher name may still have an XEmbed tray (MATE, i3): there the manager uses `java.awt.SystemTray`, as on Windows. A desktop with neither has no tray. The JDK has no D-Bus client, so the manager speaks the D-Bus wire protocol itself on the `unix:path=` socket of `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`.
- Clicks on the tray icon on Linux: each desktop decides what a click on a StatusNotifierItem does.
  Some desktops show the menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu` themselves.
  Other desktops report the click to the item: `Activate` (usually a left click), `SecondaryActivate` (usually a middle click) or `ContextMenu` (usually a right click), each with the point of the click.
  The manager answers each of these three by opening the menu of the tray icon at that point, with the same entries.
  So the click opening the menu may differ from one desktop to another (left or right, single or double), and the menu may look different for different clicks on the same desktop; every one of those clicks opens the menu.
- The bytes of a `Base16:` path: on Windows the UTF-16 code units of the name, little endian (a name may hold an unpaired surrogate); elsewhere the bytes `sun.jnu.encoding` gives, the bytes the JVM turns names into.
- Output links in Eclipse: a stack frame of the runtime reads `error line: N in file F`; a compile error reads `In file: fear:/F` and, on a later line, the numbered source line `N| ` with a line of `^` under the source line (the lines before the source line are context). `F` is relative to the project folder. The Output view fills in by pieces, so the line of a compile error is looked up when the link is used. The Output view shows a `TextConsole` in a `TextConsoleViewer`, and links text through an `IPatternMatchListener` of that console.
- Hidden buttons in Eclipse: the extension point `org.eclipse.ui.perspectiveExtensions` hides toolbar items (`hiddenToolBarItem`) and menus (`hiddenMenuItem`) by id in one perspective. The ids are those of the buttons the Eclipse IDE package shows; Window > Perspective > Customize Perspective hides items by hand, and the workbench model saved at exit lists the ids of those items.
- Syntax highlighting in Eclipse: `org.eclipse.tm4e` colours a file from a TextMate grammar. The only grammar of Fearless is `syntaxes/fearless.tmLanguage.json` of the plugin, with the themes `fearless-light.css` and `fearless-dark.css`; a rewrite keeps those files, or writes the grammar again from the tokens of the Frontend.
- JUnit view in Eclipse: `JUnitCore.importTestRunSession` of `org.eclipse.jdt.junit.core` shows a JUnit XML report file.
- Port of a message: a TCP port on the loopback address. The manager connects, sends the status line, then the reason of a refusal or the Output of the mains or of the compilation, as UTF-8 text, then closes the connection. Eclipse and a running main listen on a port the system picks.
- Each case of a main that can not have a Shortcut (a name with no matching file name, a reserved name, two mains giving the same file name and extension, a file without extension of that name) has an error of its own; the error above is the error of a name with no matching file name.
- Registering a folder whose marker has the right name and is empty writes a newline into the marker, so the marker holds what a created marker holds: GNOME never hands an empty file to the manager (see Opening a file).
- Build in Eclipse is the incremental build of Eclipse. Eclipse turns the build of a project Eclipse never built, and the build after a Clean, into a full build; a full build sends nothing, so the first Build of a project Eclipse never built sends nothing.
- The mirror of a registered project is a workspace project holding the project folder as the linked folder `src`. New Folder and New File outside `src` warn that the folder or file is outside the folder of the project, and create it anyway in the workspace.
- Eclipse waits for the end of each OS process running the manager that Eclipse starts, at most 10 seconds: an OS process running the manager that becomes the 'single' manager does not end, and that OS process running the manager writes its message files before taking the lock of `instance.lock`.
- A claim whose icon file can not be read shows, in the manager window, the icon generated from `project::pkg.Main`, as the icon of a project is generated from the project name.
- Name inference keeps the first 64 characters of the folder name turned into a valid name. Name inference skips a name `n` while the project folder holds a file other than the marker named `n.fearless` or `n`, ignoring case: on a file system ignoring case that file would be the marker, and a file without extension does not share its name with a file with an extension.
